<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishhi-Addu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ishhi-Addu was king of Qatna in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the early 18th century BCE, known from the archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a> and married his daughter to Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasmah-addu">Yasmah-Addu</a>, king at Mari. His letters show Qatna as a leading power of central Syria and a rival of Yamhad, and he was succeeded by his son Amut-pi-El II.</p></div>
